# Frontend Guidelines

## Project setup
- **Always use TypeScript** for new frontend projects (`.ts`/`.tsx`); avoid starting new projects in plain JavaScript.

## Components & structure
- Prefer functional components and hooks (React) or Composition API (Vue).
- Keep components small and single-purpose; split when > ~150 lines or multiple responsibilities.
- Colocate component-specific styles, types, and tests next to the component.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD
<button type="button" onClick={onClose} aria-label="Close dialog">
  <CloseIcon />
</button>

// ❌ BAD
<div onClick={onClose}><CloseIcon /></div>
```

## State & data
- Prefer local state first; lift only when multiple components need it.
- Use loading and error states for async UI; avoid silent failures.
- Keep form state in one place (controlled inputs or a form library).

## Styling
- Prefer design tokens (colors, spacing, typography) from a shared theme or CSS variables.
- Avoid inline styles for layout; use CSS/Sass or a utility system.
- Use relative units (rem, em) and avoid fixed px for typography when possible.

## Performance
- Lazy-load routes and heavy components with `React.lazy` / `defineAsyncComponent` (Vue).
- Memoize expensive computations and list children when needed (`useMemo`, `useCallback`, `key`).
- Optimize images (sizes, format, lazy loading) and avoid layout thrash (batch DOM reads/writes).

## Naming
- Components: PascalCase. Files: match component name (e.g. `UserProfile.tsx`).
- Handlers: `on*` or `handle*` (e.g. `onSubmit`, `handleClick`).
- Booleans: `is*`, `has*`, `should*` (e.g. `isLoading`, `hasError`).
